          %title Concrete and abstract structures
          %h1#concrete_and_abstract_structures Concrete and abstract structures
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#groups"} Groups
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#categories"} Categories
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#vector_spaces"} Vector spaces
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#hilbert_spaces"} Hilbert spaces
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#operator_algebras"} Operator algebras
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#manifolds_and_varieties"} Manifolds and varieties
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#sets"} Sets
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#points"} Points
              %li
                %a{:href => "#concrete_objects"} Concrete objects
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Many concepts from ordinary mathematics were originally developed in the course of a particular application, such that the original definition of the concept is tied to that application. The modern definition (typically some kind of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+set"} structured set
            %em concrete
            and the later notion called
            = succeed "." do
              %em abstract
          %p
            It then becomes possible to define the concrete notion in terms of the abstract one (so giving an
            %em abstract
            definition of the original
            %em concrete
            concept); usually, a concrete structure is then an abstract structure equipped with some
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+stuff"} extra stuff
            %span.newWikiWord
              representation theorem
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/representation+theorem"} ?
            showing that every abstract structure arises from some concrete structure (but sometimes this is false).
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p We list here only examples where the concrete meaning has historically been the default meaning for at least some authors or where at least one of the adjectives ‘concrete’ or ‘abstract’ have been used.
          %h3#groups Groups
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation+group"} permutation group
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutations"} permutations
            on
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            of this that includes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+function"} identity function
            and is closed under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            and taking
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+functions"} inverse functions
            %strong concrete group
            due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%89variste+Galois"} Évariste Galois
            %strong
              abstract
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            as a set equipped with an appropriate operation (taking the place of composition of permutations).
          %p
            Much of the early work on group theory dealt with symmetry groups in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            instead of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            without the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fan+theorem"} fan theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
            and so the elements of the group can still be viewed as permutations of an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying+set"} underlying set
          %p
            We can now give an
            %em abstract
            definition of the notion of
            %em concrete
            group: An abstract group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            becomes a
            %strong concrete group
            on the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            once it is given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+action"} free action
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %span.newWikiWord
              Cayley theorem
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Cayley+theorem"} ?
            shows that every group may be made into a concrete group. Of course, if we generalise from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            to an arbitrary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            (whether thought of as a category of spaces or not), then every group is
            %em already
            a concrete group, identifying a group with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+group"} automorphism group
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+category"} pointed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+category"} connected
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
          %h3#categories Categories
          %p
            Unlike groups,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            were first defined in full modern abstraction. (At least, modern for the 20th century; by the end of the 21st century, it may seem old-fashioned not to start with weak
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} categories
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bourbaki"} Bourbaki
            %strong concrete category
            as any category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+sets"} structured sets
            (as defined there).
          %p
            Of course, an
            %strong abstract category
            is the usual notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
            is a category equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful functor
            to
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+set"} structured set
            again for the equivalence.
          %h3#vector_spaces Vector spaces
          %p
            The original
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            were
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^c
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            is any
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+number"} cardinal number
            = succeed "." do
              %strong concrete vector space
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspaces"} subspaces
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^c
            closed under
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+combinations"} linear combinations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^d
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \leq c
          %p
            Then an
            = succeed "," do
              %strong abstract vector space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            thought of as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            %strong concrete vector space
            is an abstract vector space equipped with a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %h3#hilbert_spaces Hilbert spaces
          %p
            The original notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            (the one used by
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Hilbert"} David Hilbert
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2(\mathbb{R})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+space"} Lebesgue space
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            (with
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+measure"} Lebesgue measure
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2(X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+space"} measure space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete
            measure space (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            equipped with
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counting+measure"} counting measure
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^c
          %p
            Either of these (any measure space or only a discrete measure space) may be taken as a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong concrete Hilbert space
            = succeed "." do
              %strong abstract Hilbert space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthonormal+basis"} orthonormal basis
            on an abstract Hilbert space gives it the structure of a
            %strong concrete Hilbert space
            (in the stricter sense).
          %h3#operator_algebras Operator algebras
          %p
            With
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebras"} operator algebras
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            and consider the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-algebra"} algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+linear+operators"} bounded linear operators
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            to itself. A
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star-algebra"} algebra
            is a sub-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            \-algebra of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            that is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspace"} closed
            in the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norm+topology"} norm topology
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra"} von Neumann algebra
            is a sub-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            \-algebra of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            that is closed in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+operator+topology"} weak operator topology
            (a stronger condition).
          %p
            On the abstract side, a
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^*
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-star-algebra"} algebra
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+algebra"} Banach
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mo *
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x^* x\|} = {\|x\|}^2
            always holds, while a
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W-star-algebra"} algebra
            is a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^*
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predual"} predual
            as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            = succeed "-algebra," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^*
            = succeed "-algebra," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
              \-algebra
            to be a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^*
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+representation"} faithful
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            on a Hilbert space, and similarly define a
            %strong von Neumann algebra
            to be a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
          %p
            The representation theorem here is that every
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^*
            = succeed "-algebra," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            = succeed "-algebra\u2019" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^*
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
          %h3#manifolds_and_varieties Manifolds and varieties
          %p
            The original
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+varieties"} algebraic varieties
            were
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspaces"} subspaces
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+spaces"} affine spaces
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+spaces"} projective spaces
            given as the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+set"} zero set
            = succeed "s;" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+function"} algebraic function
            = succeed "." do
              %strong concrete varieties
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            = succeed "," do
              %strong abstract varieties
          %p
            Similarly,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            can be viewed as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspaces"} subspaces
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            with locally invertible local parametrisations —
            %strong> concrete manifolds
            — or as abstract sets of points equipped with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            of locally invertible local charts —
            %strong> abstract manifolds
            \. Both concepts were used from the earliest days; the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitney+embedding+theorem"} Whitney embedding theorem
            shows their equivalence (at least if the abstract manifolds are assumed to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second-countable+space"} second-countable
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff
          %h3#sets Sets
          %p
            One might view the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            as
            %strong concrete sets
            and the sets of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            as
            %strong abstract sets
            (a term used at least by
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere"} Lawvere
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+set"} pure set
            %strong concrete set
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            of a given
            = succeed ";" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                ambient set
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/ambient+set"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            as such.
          %h3#points Points
          %p
            A
            %strong
              concrete
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/element"} element
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %strong abstract point
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
            %strong concrete point
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            from an abstract point. The concrete definition of concrete point doesn't generalise from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            to arbitrary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %strong abstract point
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            %strong concrete point
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+element"} global element
          %h2#concrete_objects Concrete objects
          %p
            Since all of these concrete and abstract objects literally are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            in various
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            = succeed "\u2019," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+object"} concrete object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+categories"} concrete categories
            and so are sets with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
            … with the exception of the concrete and abstract categories themselves!).
          %p
            There is a relationship: concrete objects generalise
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+sheaves"} concrete sheaves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying+set"} underlying set
            of points, similar to how a concrete variety has a set of points from an affine or projective space. However, it's not really an example of the concept on this page, as far as I can tell.
